ets price El-Sayed around 77-81%, aligning with a clear but not safe Democratic edge", "Heavy Republican outside spending attacking El-Sayed, including ads targeting his Black support, keeps the race competitive", "Intra-party friction with Rep. Stevens in the final days adds uncertainty for Democratic turnout"], "rationale": "Polling averages cluster around a 4-5 point El-Sayed lead, expert ratings mostly call it Lean D or Tossup, and markets agree on a clear Democratic edge. Heavy outside opposition spending and the El-Sayed-Stevens feud justify staying at Lean D rather than Likely D.", "watch": "A late consolidation of Democratic factions or a shift in the outside-spending air war could move the margin several points either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Gary Peters (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Michigan's open Senate seat pits Democrat Abdul El-Sayed against Republican Mike Rogers to succeed retiring Senator Gary Peters. Late-October polls from a range of sponsors consistently show El-Sayed ahead, with most surveys in the 4-to-6-point range and even Republican-leaning pollsters finding a Democratic lead. Expert raters split between Tossup and Lean D, with a couple calling it Likely D, and prediction markets price El-Sayed's chances in the high 70s to low 80s.\n\nThe race remains competitive because of substantial outside spending against El-Sayed, including efforts to weaken his Black support, and public feuding between El-Sayed and Congresswoman Haley Stevens that Democrats are working to contain in the final stretch. Rogers has kept pace financially with help from allied groups, but the polling and market evidence points to a modest but real Democratic advantage heading into Election Day.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7769, "margin": 3.59}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7646, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7534, "margin": 3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7553, "margin": 3.4}, {"